this is the story of an evolutionary  trap a trap laid by people but which led  to the demise of thousands of  butterflies what went wrong and can we  stop it happening again we start in a  meadow in Nevada with the Edith's  Checkerspot butterfly like many  butterflies the Edith's Checkerspot has  favorite plants on which to lay its eggs  in this meadow  the butterflies first choice is called  the blue I'd marry the butterflies lay  their eggs in the spring a fortnight  later they hatch and the ravenous  caterpillars feed for two weeks before  the plant starts to die back and the  caterpillars enter a dormant state next  spring when the blue-eyed marries regrow  the caterpillars wake up gorged  themselves and build a cocoon  [Music]  then they finally emerge as a butterfly  [Music]  this cycle has been going on for  generations until humans brought in  cattle and with the cows came an  invasive plant called ribert plantain  the plantain doesn't die back in the  summer like the blue-eyed Mary  so caterpillars which hatch on the  plantain have longer to eat before  entering their dormant state helping  more of them survive to adulthood what's  more a mother butterfly with a  preference for plantain is likely to  pass that preference on to her  caterpillars the evolutionary trap was  set gradually the whole population of  butterflies became dependent on the  plantain abandoning the blue eyed Mary  entirely the butterflies did well for a  while until in 2005 the cattle ranchers  moved out without the cows grass quickly  grew engulfing the plantain and hiding  it from the butterflies without the  plantain to lay their eggs on the  butterflies were done for they couldn't  adapt fast enough to switch back to the  blue eyed Mary within a year they all  died for 4 years there were no more  Edith's Checkerspot sin the meadow but  that wasn't the end  luckily butterflies from other Meadows  which never abandoned the blue-eyed Mary  moved in and the population is now  recovering this is the first time that  this kind of evolutionary trap has been  recorded and around the world more traps  like it could already be set waiting to  go off  conservationists normally look for  problems when humans arrive but this  trap was sprung and people left it's  important to remember that humans make  changes quickly but evolution needs  longer to react and the researchers hope  that the tail of the Edith's Checkerspot  will act as a warning  you
 

